    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-47383",
      "knowledge_point_id": "167f04f0-c08e-51d8-acfa-2c4660083e6a",
      "knowledge_point": "Estimating a Derivative From a Graph Using a Forward Difference Approximation: Word Problem",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/assets/4a7e89a828cbd660357d4834e0bcb9cb5c316dbe24419865206ba5f41872963d.png)\n\nThe power of a mobile phone emitter increases when the phone is switched on. The power output of the emitter after $t$ minutes is given by $W(t),$ where $W$ is measured in watts. The graph of $W(t)$ is shown above. \nApproximate the rate of increase in the power of the emitter when $t=1$ using a forward difference approximation with step size $h=2.$",
      "worked_solution": "We use the forward difference approximation\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(t)≈\\frac{W(t+h)-W(t)}{h},\n$$\n\n \nat the time $t=1$ with step size $h=2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′}(1) & ≈\\frac{W(1+2)-W(1)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{W(3)-W(1)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{100-50}{2} \\\\  & =25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, after $1$ minute, the power is increasing at a rate of approximately $25\\,\\text{watts}/\\text{minute}.$",
